## Why This Is Asked

Assesses offline-first design, delta-based syncing, and conflict handling under intermittent connectivity, which are critical in collaboration apps used by teams.

## Key Concepts

- Core Data data model for Comment and Patch objects
- Delta/patch format including baseVersion, changes, and timestamps
- Reconciliation strategy (latest-writer-wins or server-validated merges)
- Robust test plan for offline edits, re-connect, and concurrency

## Code Example

```javascript
// Swift-like pseudocode: apply a patch to a Comment
func apply(patch: Patch, to comment: inout Comment) {
    // Validate base version matches current state
    guard patch.baseVersion == comment.version else {
        // Handle conflict resolution
        resolveConflict(patch: patch, comment: &comment)
        return
    }

    // Apply delta changes
    for change in patch.changes {
        switch change.type {
        case .insert:
            comment.text.insert(change.text, at: change.position)
        case .delete:
            comment.text.removeSubrange(change.range)
        case .replace:
            comment.text.replaceSubrange(change.range, with: change.text)
        }
    }

    // Update version and timestamps
    comment.version += 1
    comment.updatedAt = Date()
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a simple, offline-first persistence layer with Codable, basic CRUD in a SwiftUI app, and derive a user-facing metric (streak) from daily entries. It avoids external dependencies and targets beginner-friendly data modeling and UI work.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI, Codable, FileManager and Documents directory usage
- Data model design for Habit and DayEntry
- Derived metrics: computing daily streaks from consecutive entries
- Basic CRUD operations and crash-safe resume through persistent storage
- Lightweight test plan focusing on persistence and streak logic

## Code Example
```swift
struct DayEntry: Codable { let date: Date; let completed: Bool }
struct Habit: Codable { let id: UUID; var name: String; var colorHex: String; var entries: [DayEntry] }

let url = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask).first!.appendingPathComponent("habits.json")
func save(_ habits: [Habit]) throws {
  let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(habits)
  try data.write(to: url)
}
func load() -> [Habit] {
  guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: url) else { return [] }
  return (try? JSONDecoder().decode([Habit].self, from: data)) ?? []
}
```

